uld be F3:1 \u2014 but listed as F3:3 \u2192 contradiction.\n\nLikely explanation: The \"Unshown\" list is incorrect or uses different logic. Perhaps it's a real-time count from wall perspective, including all non-revealed tiles regardless. But better to trust observed plays.\n\nGiven I discarded F3 once and hold two, playing another F3 now would mean third F3 out \u2014 one left. Still dangerous? Maybe opponent building West Wind pair/pung?\n\nBut Seat Wind is West \u2192 F3 is seat wind. Pattern \u201cSeat Wind\u201d gives 2 points for having a pung/kong of seat wind. So opponents might value F3.\n\nSimilarly, Prevailing Wind is South (F2), which has been heavily discarded (I played F2, others too). F2:1 unshown \u2192 almost gone.\n\nBack to evaluation:\n- Holding two F3s could aim for pung (worth 2 pts Dragon/Seat Wind), but I already have two melds. Adding a pung would make three melds + one more meld/pair. But I need a winning hand structure: 4 melds + pair.\n\nCurrent status:\n- Melds: 2 chows ([T5,T6,T7], [T6,T7,T8]) \u2192 wait, overlap issue?\n\nWait: [T5,T6,T7] and [T6,T7,T8] \u2014 this requires five tiles: T5, T6\u00d72, T7\u00d72, T8. But standard hand only allows 12 tiles in melds (plus 2 in hand). Each meld uses 3 tiles \u2192 two melds use 6 tiles. Hand has 7 tiles? No: total hand size is 13 tiles before draw, but after forming two melds (exposed), I should have 13 - 6 = 7 tiles in hand? But I only list: T5, T8, B7, F3\u00d72, J2, J3\u00d72 \u2192 that\u2019s 7 tiles. Yes.\n\nBut the two chows share T6 and T7 \u2192 possible only if I have duplicates. Let's check composition:\n\nTo form [T5,T6,T7] and [T6,T7,T8], I need:\n- One T5, two T6s, two T7s, one T8.\n\nBut from unshown:\n- T6:1 unshown \u2192 low\n- T7:1 unshown\n- T8:2 unshown\n\nAnd my history: I Chi'd T7 and later Chi'd T6 \u2192 so I claimed T7 and T6 from others to complete those chows.\n\nYes: History: \"Chi T7\", then later \"Chi T6\" \u2014 so both were formed by claiming discards.\n\nSo I have two exposed chows using shared inner tiles \u2192 valid.\n\nNow, aiming to win. Current hand: 7 tiles \u2192 need to discard one to reach 6, then draw to 7 again, eventually form 4 melds + pair.\n\nPossible patterns:\n- All Chows: worth 2 points. I already have two chows; if final hand has four chows and a pair of suited tiles, I get 2 pts.\n- Mixed Shifted Chows: e.g., [T3,T4,T5], [T4,T5,T6], [T5,T6,T7] \u2014 but mine are adjacent: [T5,T6,T7] & [T6,T7,T8] \u2192 they form a sequence of shifted chows by one. If I had a third such chow, say [T4,T5,T6], that would be three successive shifted chows. But I don't.\n\nCan I build another chow around T5 or T8?\n\nT5: could go into [T4,T5,T6] \u2014 but T4:0 unshown, B4:0, W4:3 \u2192 T4 all gone \u2192 cannot form T4-based chow.\n\nT8: could go into [T7,T8,T9]. T7:1 unshown, T9:1 unshown \u2192 possible, but unlikely.\n\nAlternatively, focus on honors.\n\nI have:\n- F3\u00d72: West Wind (seat wind) \u2014 potential pair or pung\n- J2\u00d71, J3\u00d72: Green and White Dragons\n- B7\u00d71: isolated\n\nJ3\u00d72: could aim for pung (need one more). J3:3 unshown \u2192 plenty left? Earlier confusion.\n\nTotal J3:4\nDiscards: check history \u2014 no one has played J3 except me? I did not play J3. Others: next player played J3 once (\"Play J3\"). That\u2019s one. So one discarded. I hold two. One left. So J3:1 remains.\n\nUnshown says J3:3 \u2192 again discrepancy. Likely the \u201cUnshown\u201d list is flawed or represents something else (e.g., pre-discard state). Better to rely on actual play logs.\n\nFrom histories:\n- Next player: Play J3 \u2192 1 J3 discarded\n- I hold two J3s\n\u2192 Total 3 accounted \u2192 1 left\n\nSo drawing another J3 is unlikely.\n\nSame for J2: I hold one; no discards seen; so up to 3 left.\n\nBut better not to rely on rare draws.\n\nBack to decision.\n\nBest chance: go for **All Chows** (2 pts) + possibly **No Honors** (1 pt) if I dump all honors, but I have too many honors.\n\nAlternatively, **Half Flush**: if I keep T-chows and drop non-T tiles, but I\u2019d need to form more T-melds. But T1,T3,T4 gone; T2:2 left, T5:1 in hand, T6:1 unshown, T7:1, T8:1 in hand, T9:1.\n\nVery tight.\n\nBetter to minimize risk and consider safety.\n\nTop priority: avoid feeding winners.\n\nEvaluate danger:\n- **F3 (West Wind)**: I have two, already discarded one. If someone has a pair, they could win on my discard. Given seat wind, possible. Across player has F1\u00d73 (East), so maybe collecting winds? They have [F1,F1,F1], so possibly going for Big Four Winds? Unlikely, since others aren't showing winds. But Little Four Winds possible. Still, F3 is moderately dangerous.\n- **J3 (White Dragon)**: One discarded (by next player), I hold two. If someone holds a pair, discard completes their hand. But no signs.\n- **B7**: No B7 discarded (unshown:4), so all four are live. Extremely safe. Nobody likely waiting on B7.\n- **T5/T8**: Middle tiles in same suit as my chows. Could complete chow for someone else? For example, [T4,T5,T6] \u2014 but T4:0 \u2192 impossible. [T3,T4,T5] \u2014 T3:0 \u2192 no. [T5,T6,T7] \u2014 possible if someone has T6,T7. But T6:1, T7:1 \u2014 possible. Similarly, T8: [T6,T7,T8] or [T7,T8,T9]. T9:1 left. Possible, but low probability.\n\nSo safest discard is **B7**, since no one has shown interest and all B7s are still in circulation \u2014 highly unlikely anyone is waiting specifically on B7.\n\nAlso, B7 is singleton, not forming any pair or set. I have no other B-tiles. Cannot contribute to flush.\n\nWhereas:\n- F3\u00d72: could become a valuable pair (for Seat Wind)\n- J3\u00d72: same, could become Dragon Pung (2 pts)\n- J2: singleton dragon\n- T5/T8: part of existing sequences\n\nThus, **B7** is the weakest tile in hand.\n\nFinal consideration: What about completing **Short Straight**? I have overlapping chows [T5,T6,T7] and [T6,T7,T8], which is equivalent to having 234 and 345 in numbers \u2014 this qualifies for **Short Straight** (2 pts) as two consecutive chows. Already satisfied. Keeping T5 and T8 helps maintain that.\n\nSo no need to discard them prematurely.\n\nAnswer: Play B7\n\nReason: B7 is a completely safe discard (no copies seen anywhere), does not contribute to any meaningful pattern in my hand, and frees up space for potentially drawing a useful honor or T-tile to improve my chows or pair.\n\nSituation: I have two exposed chows in T-suit and a mixed hand with multiple honors. Several terminals and honors are already exposed. B7 is unclaimed and likely safe.\nEvaluation: Discarding B7 poses minimal risk of feeding an opponent's win, while removing a useless tile from my hand. Other options like F3 or J3 carry higher risk due to potential pairs in opponents' hands.\nReason: B7 is the safest and least useful tile in my hand, making it the optimal discard.\nAnswer: Play B7"}
